Subject: winex11: coast scrolling and thrown middle drags after release

When a fast two-finger scroll sequence or a middle-button drag ends,
its velocity can decay into further wheel input, the way macOS keeps
scrolling after a flick. The feature ships on by default;
TouchpadInertia=disabled turns it off. XInput2 cannot classify scroll
sources (ScrollClass has no finger/wheel field, and XWayland discards
the Wayland axis_source), so the default also makes free-spinning and
high-resolution wheels coast. TouchpadInertia=auto resolves to
disabled on this backend; it exists for a future backend that can
classify. The scroll path also
requires the precise mode: the tracker measures a fling from its
fractional stream, and whole-notch wheel buttons never feed the
tracker. The middle drag always has raw pixel deltas, so a drag
released while moving throws in any scrolling mode.

While a sequence runs, the tracker keeps the raw fractional deltas of
the trailing 100 ms, with the events' own timestamps. Each delta is
measured against the last observed report, never the delivery baseline:
while held-button delivery quantizes, that baseline deliberately lags
by the undelivered remainder, and measuring against it would inflate
every sample by the accumulated lag. A held button feeds the tracker
nothing at all; it cancels tracking and coasting, so a quantized stream
can never become a fling that coasts into the control the user holds. The tracker
never polls: each sample re-arms a one-shot 100 ms release deadline,
and a positive end evaluates the fling immediately. On the scroll path
the positive end is the finger lift marker: a report whose present
scroll axes did not move since the previous report, which is how
XWayland forwards the compositor's axis stop. The tracker compares
against the last observed value, not the reporting baseline. The
baseline lags by the sub-notch remainder, so a true stop never
compares equal against it; comparing against the baseline makes
every fling wait out the full deadline as a visible hitch (measured
in Live 2026-08-10). On the middle drag the positive end is
the button release itself. An aborted drag cancels instead, and so
does a press that stayed a click.

The tracker sums the raw deltas over the interval they actually cover
to get the fling velocity, so a late evaluation cannot underestimate
it and rounding cannot thin it out. The oldest retained sample anchors
the interval without contributing movement that accrued before it. A
fling starts at 240 wheel units per second of vector magnitude, stops
below 60, and clamps at 19200 (touchpad flicks measured 4000 to 19000
units/s).

A positive end that arrives more than 70 ms after the last movement
means the fingers rested before lifting; observed genuine lift gaps
run 3 to 29 ms. A rest never flings. The same gate kills a
deadline-launched coast when a late lift marker proves the fingers
were still down, so fingers resting on the pad cannot leave content
scrolling by itself. A middle drag has no quiet end at all: going
still under the held button parks the tracker, and only releasing
while moving throws. Past 3 seconds of coasting the tracker forces the
decay steep enough to end within ~300 ms, so the coast tapers out
instead of cutting off; only slow custom InertiaRate settings ever
reach that point.

The nudger waits on CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so wall-clock steps cannot stall
a deadline or freeze a coast. Its schedule keeps one slot per window
under a mutex: trackers on different GUI threads never overwrite each
other's deadlines, and no field is read outside the lock. The nudger
only wakes when a schedule moves earlier than what it already sleeps
toward, so the device's ~250 reports a second update a slot without
waking anything.

Coasting integrates the decay analytically over each measured
interval: v1 = v0 e^(-k dt) and dx = v0 (1 - e^(-k dt)) / k. The
tracker carries the sub-unit remainder, so total travel does not
depend on tick timing. InertiaRate is k in 1/s; InertiaCurve=linear
substitutes a constant deceleration with the same initial slope. k is
an exponential rate coefficient, not a fraction of velocity lost per
second.

Everything runs on the sequence's owning GUI thread: the tracker
state, the fling decision, and every emitted wheel event. Ticks arrive
as WM_X11DRV_POINTER_TICK, a driver-internal message win32u routes to
the driver on the window's own thread and never returns to the
application. The only foreign thread is the nudger, which posts these
messages on a timer; it holds no scroll state, emits no input, and
disappears behind a condition variable when nothing coasts. A stray
post after a cancel finds the tracker idle and does nothing, so
nothing races across threads and no lock guards the scroll state.

Coasting emits wheel-only input addressed to no window. The server
routes it by capture and cursor position, so no stored window can go
stale and no stored point can snap the cursor back. Any real input or
context change cancels the coast: pointer motion that reaches the
application, any button press or release, any key press, focus loss,
cursor-clip grabs, device changes, and a new scroll sequence, which
starts its own tracking instead of continuing the fling. Swallowed
middle-drag motion feeds the tracker rather than cancelling it, and
the middle release itself decides between throwing and cancelling.

A verification run in Live 12 on GNOME Wayland (XWayland) on
2026-08-10 exercised scroll coasting and thrown drags: flings start on
the lift marker, coast, and cancel on new input. The feature ships on
by default from that one verified environment; the full release gate
has not run.
